{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "My house is in a historic district. Can I still remodel the bathroom?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Almost always, yes, and usually without the preservation process entering into it. The city requires a Certificate of Appropriateness before exterior work begins and before a building permit can be issued for a property in a district, and a bathroom remodel is interior work. Where it does apply is anything that changes the outside of the house: a new or altered window, an exterior door, or venting through an outside wall. We confirm your address against the city's historic district map before writing a scope."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "I heard Florida changed the law and I do not need a permit any more. Is that right?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Not for a project like this. House Bill 803 exempts certain residential projects valued under $7,500, but the city is explicit that the exemption is not automatic and that you must apply for and receive approval before starting work. A full bathroom renovation costs well above that threshold, so it rarely applies to the work we do."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "Are you actually based in Lake Worth Beach?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Yes. Our address is 1730 N Federal Hwy, Lake Worth Beach, FL 33460. It means the first walkthrough is easy to schedule, and it means the gaps between stages of your project are not driving time."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "The house is old. What are you likely to find behind the wall?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "In this city, often cast iron waste lines near the end of their life, galvanized supply, and electrical never sized for a modern bathroom. We look during the walkthrough and price it into the estimate rather than issuing a change order on day three."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "Can you build the bathroom so it still works as we get older?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Yes, and the time to decide is at framing rather than later. Blocking behind the wall for a future grab bar, a wider door opening, and a curbless or low-threshold shower entry are inexpensive while the wall is open and expensive once it is tiled. We raise it during design even when nobody has asked."
}
},
{
